import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps on the Mac — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same comparison as last time, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos gives every decode to Apple's ImageIO, and LightCraft does all of them in its own Rust. That changes which app is faster, and where."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps on the Mac</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same comparison as <a
          href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">last
          time</a>, on the desktop. On the Mac, LightPhotos gives every
          decode to Apple's ImageIO.
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          does all of them in its own Rust. That changes which app is faster,
          and where.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 8 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          On the same twenty Sony RAW files, LightPhotos filled its grid in
          0.73&nbsp;s, and LightCraft took 1.24&nbsp;s. LightPhotos used
          0.9&nbsp;GB of memory to LightCraft's 1.7&nbsp;GB, from a
          14&nbsp;MB app to a 43&nbsp;MB app. The next photo went the other
          way. Both apps reacted in about 0.35&nbsp;s. But LightCraft was
          finished in 0.38&nbsp;s, and LightPhotos took 1.1&nbsp;s.
          LightCraft fully develops the photos on either side of the one you
          are looking at. LightPhotos only gets their embedded JPEGs ready.
          It asks ImageIO to develop the RAW data when you arrive.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Two decode paths to the same screen. A photo file, JPEG, HEIC or RAW, goes to each app. LightPhotos gives every decode to Apple's ImageIO, which is part of macOS, so the decoders cost the app nothing. LightCraft decodes with its own Rust code: a RAW decoder, demosaic, JPEG and JPEG XL. Both apps draw with wgpu on Metal. LightPhotos is 14 MB installed, and its colours are the way Apple develops them. LightCraft is 43 MB installed, and its colours are the same on every platform.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="0" width="320" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="176" y="21">Your photo file</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="176" y="39">JPEG, HEIC or RAW, read in place</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M220 48 V62 H150 V80"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M145 80 L155 80 L150 88 Z"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M420 48 V62 H490 V80"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M485 80 L495 80 L490 88 Z"></path>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="78">LIGHTPHOTOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="88" width="300" height="76" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="112">Apple's ImageIO, part of macOS</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="132">Every decode goes to the OS</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="150">The decoders cost the app nothing</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="340" y="78">LIGHTCRAFT</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="340" y="88" width="300" height="76" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="356" y="112">Its own decoders, in Rust</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="356" y="132">RAW decoder, demosaic, JPEG, JPEG XL</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="356" y="150">The same code on every platform</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M150 164 V182 H220 V192"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M215 192 L225 192 L220 200 Z"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M490 164 V182 H420 V192"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M415 192 L425 192 L420 200 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="200" width="320" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="176" y="221">The same screen</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="176" y="239">Both apps draw with wgpu on Metal</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="274">App, installed: 14 MB</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="292">Colours: the way Apple develops them</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="340" y="274">App, installed: 43 MB</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="340" y="292">Colours: the same on every platform</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          On the Mac, the two apps differ in one main place: the decoder.
          LightPhotos uses the decoders that come with macOS. LightCraft
          brings its own. Most of the size, speed and memory differences
          below follow from that choice.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The next-photo result reversed.</strong> In the browser,
          LightPhotos finished the next photo in 0.53&nbsp;s, and LightCraft
          in 1.88&nbsp;s. On the Mac, LightCraft finishes in 0.38&nbsp;s, and
          LightPhotos in 1.1&nbsp;s. But both apps put something on screen in
          about 0.35&nbsp;s. A Retina window needs more pixels than the
          camera's JPEG has. So on the Mac, LightPhotos develops the RAW data
          too, and it does that only when you arrive.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Two ways to step through a shoot.</strong> LightCraft
          develops the next and previous photos while you look at the current
          one. So each step is finished the moment you arrive. LightPhotos
          only does the work for the photo you are on. It shows something
          just as fast, and it does less work that you never see.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ImageIO is still worth it.</strong> It makes LightPhotos
          small and makes its thumbnails nearly free. It also reads HEIC and
          Canon CR3 files, which LightCraft cannot fully decode yet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What changes on the desktop</h2>
      <p>
        In the browser, the interesting differences were about threads,
        workers and storage. On the desktop, those differences mostly go
        away. Both apps are ordinary Rust programs that use every core and
        draw with <code>wgpu</code> on Metal. The difference that remains is
        the one that matters most for a photo app: where the pixels come
        from.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>LightPhotos uses ImageIO for everything.</strong> On the
          Mac, every JPEG, HEIC and RAW file goes through Apple's ImageIO.
          Preview and Photos use the same library. The Mac build does not
          even compile in the RAW library that the other builds use. I wrote
          about what that one call does in <a
          href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">What ImageIO gives
          you free</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft brings its own decoders.</strong> It runs the
          same decoders on the Mac as everywhere else. These are its own RAW
          decoder and demosaic, and Rust libraries for JPEG, PNG, TIFF, WebP
          and JPEG XL. A photo looks the same on every platform, and
          LightCraft does not depend on what Apple supports.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Both read your folders in place.</strong> LightPhotos opens
          a folder and keeps its thumbnail cache beside the photos.
          LightCraft adds the photos to a library catalog. It does not copy
          them unless you ask it to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft has far more finished features.</strong>
          LightPhotos is for going through a folder fast, keeping the good
          photos and giving them quick edits. Much of what a full editor does
          is not there yet. LightCraft is already a full Lightroom-style
          editor. It has masks, tone curves, colour grading, healing, lens
          corrections, presets, versions and history. It also has HDR and
          panorama merges, albums and stacks, and export to six formats.
          Keep that in mind with every number below.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Decoding</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Apple's ImageIO, for every format</td>
              <td>Its own, in Rust, for every format</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">A RAW at fit-to-window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">The embedded JPEG, then ImageIO's develop at the window's size</td>
              <td>Its own develop, up to 2560&nbsp;px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The photos on either side</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Embedded JPEG ready</td>
              <td>Fully developed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HEIC</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Not yet</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Canon CR3</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Embedded preview only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">A folder, read in place</td>
              <td>A library catalog, files stay in place</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, with no copyleft libraries in the Mac app</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built both apps from their current code. For each one, I used the
        project's own release script, the one that makes the download. I ran
        both on the same M4 Pro Mac with 48&nbsp;GB of memory, on macOS
        26.3. The photos were the same twenty Sony A7 II RAW files as last
        time. I copied them to a new folder for every run, so that no cache
        had seen them. Each app also got a fresh home folder, so it started
        with its default settings and a window of 1600 by 1000 points.
      </p>
      <p>
        The method is the browser one, moved to the desktop. A script
        launches the app on the folder and double-clicks the first photo.
        Then it presses the right arrow eight times. It captures the window
        every 150&nbsp;ms. It records a step as done when nothing changes
        for three seconds. This time it also records the first capture that
        changed. So you can see when the app reacted, and when it finished.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each number is the median of five runs. The runs agreed to within a
        few percent. The one exception was a LightCraft open that took
        0.86&nbsp;s. Every capture takes about 0.23&nbsp;s, so no
        measurement can be below about 0.35&nbsp;s. The script is
        <code>tools/desktop-bench</code>. It only looks at the window, so it
        works on any Mac app.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download (.dmg)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">7.3&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>15.2&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">App, installed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">14&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>43&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.31&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.43&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to finished grid, 20 RAWs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.73&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.24&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open the first photo: reacts / finished</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.42 / 1.12&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.48 / 0.51&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Next photo, each (median of 8): reacts / finished</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.35 / 1.11&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.37 / 0.38&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.9&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>1.7&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 248" role="img" aria-label="Time in seconds. Launch to finished grid: LightPhotos 0.73, LightCraft 1.24. Next photo, reacts: LightPhotos 0.35, LightCraft 0.37. Next photo, finished: LightPhotos 1.11, LightCraft 0.38.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SECONDS, LOWER IS FASTER</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="46">Launch to finished grid</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="66">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="58" width="234" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="362" y="67">0.73</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="86">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="78" width="400" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="528" y="87">1.24</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="122">Next photo, reacts</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="142">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="134" width="113" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="241" y="143">0.35</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="162">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="154" width="119" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="247" y="163">0.37</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="198">Next photo, finished</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="218">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="210" width="358" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="486" y="219">1.11</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="238">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="230" width="123" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="251" y="239">0.38</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        One note on the grid. LightCraft opens its grid scrolled to the end.
        At this window size, 11 of the 20 thumbnails were on screen, so its
        1.24&nbsp;s is for those 11. LightPhotos showed all 20.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the next photo reversed</h2>
      <p>
        For this part, I turned on the timing logs in both apps. When you
        step to a photo, LightPhotos shows the JPEG that the camera embedded
        in the RAW file. It does this within about 20&nbsp;ms. That is why
        both apps react at the same time. In both, the first change you see
        is at the lowest time the script can measure.
      </p>
      <p>
        But the A7 II's embedded JPEG is only 1616&nbsp;px wide. On a Retina
        screen, the photo area of this window is 3584&nbsp;px wide. So
        LightPhotos then asks ImageIO to develop the RAW data at that size,
        which took 0.5 to 0.6&nbsp;s per photo. It already has the embedded
        JPEGs of the photos on either side. But it does not start that
        develop until you arrive, so every step pays for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft develops the RAW data itself, at up to 2560&nbsp;px. That
        is about half as many pixels. When a photo is on screen, LightCraft
        decodes and develops the next and previous ones in the background
        and keeps them ready. Pressing the arrow then costs only a GPU pass
        of about 35&nbsp;ms. That is why its 0.38&nbsp;s is right at the
        lowest time the script can measure.
      </p>
      <p>
        My script spends a few seconds on each photo, which gives LightCraft
        time to get ahead. That is about the pace of a person who looks at
        each photo. If you hold the arrow key down, you could move faster
        than LightCraft can prepare. I did not measure that.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not think either is the wrong choice. When LightCraft prepares
        both neighbours, it throws away at least one of the two develops on
        every step. When you hold the arrow key or jump around the grid, it
        throws away most of them. That is CPU time and battery spent on
        photos you never look at.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meanwhile, LightPhotos has something on screen in the same time as
        LightCraft. The camera's JPEG is plenty to judge framing and
        expression, which is most of culling. In the half second after that,
        the photo sharpens to full Retina detail while you already look at
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos could prepare its neighbours too. The memory cost would
        be lower than for LightCraft, because LightPhotos keeps finished
        8-bit images. These are about 34&nbsp;MB each at this size, so about
        70&nbsp;MB for two. Whether that is worth the wasted develops depends
        on how people move through a folder. I want to measure that before I
        change anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the grid and the memory go the other way</h2>
      <p>
        For a grid thumbnail, LightPhotos asks ImageIO for an image no
        bigger than the thumbnail. ImageIO answers from the JPEG embedded in
        the RAW file. That took 12 to 22&nbsp;ms per file here. That is how
        twenty RAW files fill the grid in under a second. LightCraft also
        shows each file's embedded JPEG first. Then it replaces the JPEG with
        its own thumbnail, which it develops from the RAW data. Its grid is
        not finished until those thumbnails arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Part of the memory gap comes from preparing ahead. Part comes from
        what each app keeps. LightCraft keeps the decoded RAW data for the
        current photo and both neighbours. It keeps this data as 32-bit
        floating-point RGB, at 12 bytes a pixel. That is about 52&nbsp;MB
        for each photo at 2560&nbsp;px.
      </p>
      <p>
        This data lets the LightCraft sliders work on the full range of the
        sensor. It keeps highlights above white, and it lets exposure change
        without banding. On the Mac, LightPhotos gets finished 8-bit images
        from ImageIO, at 4 bytes a pixel. For the neighbours, it keeps only
        their small embedded JPEGs. On screen, the two look the same. But
        those sources only explain about 150&nbsp;MB of the 0.8&nbsp;GB
        difference, and I did not break down the rest.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app size is ImageIO again. LightCraft ships its own decoders for
        RAW, JPEG, JPEG XL and more, plus its own colour management. Its
        release build also skips link-time optimisation and keeps some debug
        information. LightPhotos uses the decoders that already come with
        macOS, so they cost it nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>What ImageIO costs</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO is not free in every sense. The photos look the way Apple
        develops them. That is not the same as LightPhotos on Linux, on
        Windows or in the browser. LightPhotos only supports the cameras that
        Apple supports, when Apple supports them. And when ImageIO decodes a
        file incorrectly, I cannot fix it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft gets the opposite trade. It has one decoder and one
        develop on every platform. So the same photo looks the same
        everywhere, and LightCraft can fix any bug in its decoder. The cost
        is a bigger app and the formats it has not written yet. HEIC needs
        an HEVC decoder. There is no pure-Rust HEVC decoder with a permissive
        license yet. CR3 files show only their embedded preview for now.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        On the Mac, LightPhotos relies on the operating system, and
        LightCraft relies on itself. The numbers follow from that.
        LightPhotos is smaller and starts faster. It fills its grid faster
        and uses less memory, because Apple already did that work. Part of
        the reason is also that LightPhotos simply does less. A much smaller
        feature set fits in a smaller app.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft is faster where it chose to do more work ahead of time.
        It looks the same on every platform. And it does far more when you
        start to edit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stepping through photos shows the same split on a small scale.
        LightCraft spends memory and work early, so every step is finished
        when you arrive. LightPhotos does only what you ask for. It shows the
        camera's JPEG while it finishes the rest. Both feel fast, for
        different reasons.
      </p>

